;;; A query's claim that lists a fact of a relation only a rule's `:assert`
;;; derives is refused —
;;; M1f **W78**, register row `W78/claim-derived-relation`, filed by S1f.7.11 (T1f.7.11.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, of whom Ann has paid, no candidate, and a rule that admits whoever
;;; has paid. `admitted` is declared nowhere and no fact is written of it: the
;;; rule's `:assert` is what makes it a relation. The goal asks who is admitted,
;;; and the claim says Ann.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; R is root's state alone, which holds `(admitted Ann)` by the rule. The goal's
;;; one row is `?g = Ann`, its class `(admitted Ann)`, and the claim holds, exit 0.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `73a0c17d`, release: refused at load, *:expect names admitted, which no
;;; declaration or fact makes a relation*, exit 1 on every command, where the
;;; goal alone loaded and answered `?g = Ann`.
;;; A claim's relation is a relation of the program, one a rule's `:assert` writes
;;; among them, since S1f.7.11 (T1f.7.11.3): the claim loads and holds.

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(rule welcome ()
  :match  (paid ?g)
  :assert (admitted ?g)
  :why "{?g} has paid, so {?g} is admitted")
(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(paid Ann)
(solve :hypothesis-relations ())
(query :goal (admitted ?g) :expect (model (admitted Ann)))
